-- title:
--   Effective polynomial saturation and length of finite contact ideals
-- statement:
--   Let $g_2,g_3\in\mathbb C$, let $A=\mathbb C[t,x_1,x_2,x_3]$, and choose either of the elliptic-extension chart derivations
--
--   $$D_0=\partial_t+x_2\partial_{x_1}+(6x_1^2-g_2/2)\partial_{x_2}-x_1\partial_{x_3},$$
--
--   $$D_1=\partial_t+(-6x_2^2+(g_2/2)x_1^2)\partial_{x_1}+(-1/2-g_2x_1x_2-(3g_3/2)x_1^2)\partial_{x_2}+(-2g_2x_2^2-3g_3x_1x_2)\partial_{x_3}.$$
--
--   Write $D=D_c$. For each $v\in\mathbb C^4$ and $n\in\mathbb N$, let $C(v,n)$ be the chart contact ideal generated by polynomials whose first $n$ $D$-jets vanish at $v$.
--
--   Let $V\subseteq\mathbb C^4$ be finite, let $n_v\in\mathbb N$, and set
--
--   $$I=\bigcap_{v\in V}C(v,n_v).$$
--
--   For a polynomial $p\in A$, choose any $N\in\mathbb N$ with $n_v\le N$ for all $v\in V$. Define $e_v=0$ if $p(v)=0$ and $e_v=n_v$ otherwise. Write $I:p^N=\{q\in A:q p^N\in I\}$ and $I:p^\infty=\{q\in A:\exists r\in\mathbb N,\ q p^r\in I\}$. Then
--
--   $$I:p^N=I:p^\infty=\bigcap_{v\in V} C(v,e_v).$$
--
--   Moreover, the saturated quotient is finite dimensional over $\mathbb C$ and
--
--   $$\dim_{\mathbb C} A/(I:p^N)=\sum_{\substack{v\in V\\p(v)\ne0}} n_v.$$
--
--   Thus saturation removes precisely the components on which $p$ vanishes and retains the full contact orders at every other point. Every exponent at least the largest contact order gives the same ideal. This provides a finite algebraic way to restrict the contact scheme to the locus $p\ne0$, while retaining its exact length.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The theorem is unconditional for the defined chart derivations and contact ideals, apart from the displayed exponent bound. Empty $V$, zero orders and the zero polynomial are included. Order-zero factors are the whole ring; an empty intersection is the whole ring and has quotient length zero. No first-coordinate injectivity, time-presentation, finite-dimensionality or nonsingularity hypothesis is assumed. The formal statement expresses the saturation equality by an equivalence for each polynomial $q$, without introducing a separate saturation definition. In Lean, `I.colon {p ^ N}` denotes the colon by the singleton element $p^N$. Chart indices 0 and 1 correspond to the homogeneous-coordinate charts $S_0$ and $S_2$. This is a finite-contact calculation, without a global algebraic-group estimate.
-- source:
--   Derived finite-contact saturation calculation for the commutative-algebra method associated with Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A, https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. Uses the proved contact structure and quotient dimension theorems. A polynomial vanishing at a point has its Nth power in the contact ideal for N at least the contact order; away from that zero locus, primarity cancels every power. This yields the effective saturation and its exact length. Not quoted as Theorem A.2. Primary formal references: Ideal.isPrimary_iff, Ideal.pow_mem_pow, Ideal.pow_le_pow_right, Submodule.mem_colon_singleton and Submodule.mem_iInf.

import Definitions.Def_WeierstrassEllipticZeta_ProjectiveContactIdeal
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Colon
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.IsPrimary
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Quotient.Basic
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.FiniteDimensional.Defs

open WeierstrassEllipticZeta
open scoped Classical

theorem WeierstrassEllipticZeta.finite_contact_saturation_length (g₂ g₃ : ℂ) (c : Fin 2)
    (V : Finset (Fin 4 → ℂ)) (n : V → ℕ) (p : MvPolynomial (Fin 4) ℂ)
    (N : ℕ) (hN : ∀ v : V, n v ≤ N) :
    let I : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Fin 4) ℂ) :=
      ⨅ v : V, extensionChartContactIdeal g₂ g₃ c v.val (n v)
    let e : V → ℕ := fun v => if MvPolynomial.eval v.val p = 0 then 0 else n v
    let J : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Fin 4) ℂ) :=
      ⨅ v : V, extensionChartContactIdeal g₂ g₃ c v.val (e v)
    I.colon {p ^ N} = J ∧
      (∀ q : MvPolynomial (Fin 4) ℂ, (∃ r : ℕ, q * p ^ r ∈ I) ↔ q ∈ J) ∧
      FiniteDimensional ℂ (MvPolynomial (Fin 4) ℂ ⧸ I.colon {p ^ N}) ∧
      Module.finrank ℂ (MvPolynomial (Fin 4) ℂ ⧸ I.colon {p ^ N}) = ∑ v : V, e v := by sorry
